  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 PM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n- Showers and Slight Chance Thunderstorms into Thursday Morning\n\nThe chance for occasional showers and perhaps a thunderstorm \ncontinues into Thursday morning, though the potential for heavy \nrain should likely be contained south of southwest Michigan \ntonight. Low and midlevel cloud cover and patches of light rain \nstretch from southern Michigan into Iowa. A train of midlevel \nmesoscale vorticity centers has been evident on radar and \nsatellite among these clouds along the effective midlevel front, \nand will be the primary impetus for showers this afternoon. 850 mb\nair parcels are modeled to be weakly unstable, though convective \ntops may struggle to reach -10 C, which supports showers but with \na limited chance of lightning.\n\nTonight into Thursday morning, a southwesterly low-level jet \nnoses into Iowa and becomes more of a westerly LLJ over northern \nIllinois and Indiana. Thunderstorm development will be focused \nover this region along the effective midlevel front, as the \nconvection-allowing models have been suggesting. A few showers \nmay still develop over Lower Michigan from weakly unstable cloud \nlayers between 850 and 700 mb under a slight uptick in more \nfavorable upper- level jet dynamics. The ECMWF ensemble has been \nmost aggressive in providing a soaking rain to Lower Michigan (the\n00 Z ensemble even gave GRR a median QPF of 1 inch, though the 06\nZ run did back off some). However, the GEFS and CMC ensemble \nsystem along with the CAMs have been keeping the soaking rains \nsouth of Michigan, which I am more inclined to believe.\n\n- Heavy Rain Possible Saturday\n\nLate Thursday into Friday is favored to be dry as the front moves\nfarther south while surface high pressure scoots across northern \nOntario. Rain chances then become highly likely from late Friday \nnight into a portion of Saturday. The front well to our south and \nsouthwest will surge back northeast as a warm front, approaching \nsouthwest Michigan, with a 35 knot low level jet ascending up the \nfrontal surface. PWAT values around 2 inches and weak instability \nwill also be present.\n\nThe global ensemble systems have generally been producing median \nQPF around 1 inch, with about 20 percent reaching 2 inches, \nindicating a widespread soaking rain is likely along with swaths \nof locally more than 2 inches where thunderstorms persist. There \nwill remain some question for now regarding where the axis of \nheavier rain will fall, whether it be more so in central Michigan \nor in southern Michigan, as it will depend on the latitude of the \nlow pressure development and its associated warm frontal surge, \nand whether the thunderstorm complexes may preferentially \npropagate farther south into the better instability compared to \nwhere the global models place them.",
